Tenuis (Latin; thin, precise) is a small and lightweight CISC word machine. 
Tenuis is designed to be fast and lightweight, running on machines with 
very little memory, while still being suited to the needs of 
higher-performance computing. Tenuis achieves this by trying to perform as 
little unneeded work per instruction and need as few instructions as 
possible to perform a logical operation.

Please note, this is not close to a release. This is very early.

To build software which uses libt (the native C library), include the
directories tools/cc/lib/libt/ and tools/cc/lib/libt/h/libt/ using the -I
flag in the C preprocessor. ANSI C is (kind-of) supported via the
libraries in tools/cc/lib/libc/ and tools/cc/lib/libc/h/. Please include
these, as well as add #include <ansi.h> or add the -ansi flag to the
preprocessor step if the software being compiled needs ANSI C.

Some libt libraries have pieces of them in Assembler and require merging
with the compiled source code. A helper to compile code easily should be
present in bin/compile to handle inclusions and merging easily. This can
be used simply as

	./bin/compile file.c file.bin

where file.c is the C language file and file.bin is the output bytecode.




